{"open_points":[{"subject":"CUSTOMER_VALUE_FAILURE_KINDS","kind":"undefined constant","text":"CUSTOMER_VALUE_FAILURE_KINDS","question":"What fact does this constant contain, what are its members and type, and is it authoritative or derived from the customer-value rules and their failure-kind enumeration?","options":["An immutable set of every failure kind customer-value validation may return","A mapping from customer fields or checks to failure kinds","A derived value that should not be an independently owned constant"],"default_guess":"Make it an immutable set derived from the customer-value validation specification, with data_provider owning the set and models owning the failure-kind enumeration.","witness":{"initial":"A customer submission contains a blank required name and an out-of-form kind.","events":["The owner submits the customer form.","Customer-value validation classifies the failures using CUSTOMER_VALUE_FAILURE_KINDS."],"outcomes":["One implementation treats the constant as the allowed failure kinds and returns both classified failures.","Another treats it as a field-to-kind mapping or an incomplete subset and returns a different kind or omits one failure."],"observer":"The owner sees a different refusal answer."}},{"subject":"BACKUP_NONCE_BYTES","kind":"missing value and contract","text":"BACKUP_NONCE_BYTES","question":"How many bytes is a backup nonce, how is it generated and stored, and which encryption operation consumes it?","options":["A fixed random nonce length for each encrypted backup","A nonce length determined by the selected encryption algorithm","A format field owned by the backup backend rather than data_provider"],"default_guess":"Use a fresh operating-system-random nonce per backup, with the exact length fixed by the chosen authenticated-encryption algorithm and owned as a data_provider constant."},{"subject":"BACKUP_KEY_BYTES","kind":"ambiguous key meaning","text":"BACKUP_KEY_BYTES","question":"Does this mean the required length of the configured backup key, the length of a derived binary encryption key, or something else, and how does it coexist with A17 accepting every configured backup key of at least SECRET_MIN_BYTES?","options":["Exact length of the derived binary encryption key","Exact length required of the configured UTF-8 backup secret","Minimum configured-secret length duplicating SECRET_MIN_BYTES"],"default_guess":"Treat it as the exact length of the derived binary encryption key; continue accepting configured backup secrets of any UTF-8 length at least SECRET_MIN_BYTES.","witness":{"initial":"The owner configures a 64-byte UTF-8 backup key, which satisfies A17's minimum of 32 bytes.","events":["Registry starts and validates the configuration.","A backup run derives or consumes its encryption key."],"outcomes":["An implementation reading BACKUP_KEY_BYTES as a derived-key size accepts the configuration and derives that many bytes.","An implementation reading it as the configured-key size rejects the start or truncates the configured secret."],"observer":"The owner sees Registry either start normally or fail/change how the backup key is used."}},{"subject":"BACKUP_KEY_DERIVATION_HASH","kind":"undefined cryptographic choice","text":"BACKUP_KEY_DERIVATION_HASH","question":"Which hash algorithm does this name, which key-derivation construction uses it, and is the value an algorithm identifier, callable, or model enumeration member?","options":["A release-fixed hash identifier used by the backup KDF","A hash implementation selected by the backup backend","The same algorithm as DIGEST_ALGORITHM or BACKUP_KEY_DERIVATION_HASH as a distinct choice"],"default_guess":"Define an explicit supported hash identifier for the backup KDF, distinct from DIGEST_ALGORITHM unless the text deliberately makes them the same, and have the backup backend consume it."},{"subject":"PROJECT_STATUS_CONFIRMATIONS","kind":"undefined classification","text":"PROJECT_STATUS_CONFIRMATIONS","question":"What are the keys and values of this constant: project statuses requiring confirmation, project transitions requiring confirmation, or owner actions requiring confirmation, and which actions are members?","options":["A set of project statuses whose entry requires confirmation","A set of project lifecycle transitions requiring confirmation","A mapping from owner actions to the project statuses against which they are confirmable"],"default_guess":"Represent confirmation requirements by owner action or lifecycle transition, including archive, anonymise and delete-whole, rather than by status alone.","witness":{"initial":"An archived project is shown to the owner for anonymisation or deletion whole.","events":["The owner submits the selected erasure action.","The interface decides from PROJECT_STATUS_CONFIRMATIONS whether to show a confirmation step."],"outcomes":["An action-based interpretation requires confirmation for both erasure kinds.","A status-based interpretation may treat every action on an archived project alike or fail to distinguish the two erasure actions."],"observer":"The owner either receives the required confirmation step or the action follows a different confirmation path."}},{"subject":"OWNER_NAVIGATION_ACTIONS and OWNER_CHANGE_ACTIONS","kind":"duplicate authority and dependency direction","text":"OWNER_NAVIGATION_ACTIONS
OWNER_CHANGE_ACTIONS","question":"How are these sets defined, are they authoritative or derived from OWNER_ACTIONS, and if they disagree with OwnerActionRoute.is_change which fact wins?","options":["Derived partitions of OWNER_ACTIONS using OwnerActionRoute.is_change","Independent authoritative sets from which OWNER_ACTIONS rows are validated or built","Navigation and change overlap, with separately stated membership rules"],"default_guess":"Make OWNER_ACTIONS the authority and derive disjoint navigation/change partitions from each row's is_change field; reject any release where the partitions do not exactly match.","witness":{"initial":"An OWNER_ACTIONS row marks an owner action as not a change, while OWNER_CHANGE_ACTIONS contains it.","events":["The owner opens and submits that action.","The router and CSRF handling classify it."],"outcomes":["An implementation trusting OWNER_ACTIONS treats it as navigation and does not run the change path.","An implementation trusting OWNER_CHANGE_ACTIONS treats it as a change and applies change-only checks or handling."],"observer":"The owner sees different routing, CSRF, or state-change behaviour."}},{"subject":"SERVICE_CHANGE_OPERATIONS","kind":"undefined operation partition","text":"SERVICE_CHANGE_OPERATIONS","question":"Which service operations are members, what definition of “change” is used, and is this set authoritative or derived from operation contracts or manifest effect classes?","options":["Every service operation that mutates Registry state","Only operations whose manifest effect class is state-transition","An independently maintained subset used for transaction or authorization handling"],"default_guess":"Derive it from the operation contracts as every service-API operation that mutates Registry state, and validate it against the manifest effect classes rather than maintaining a second authority.","witness":{"initial":"Cabinet Kernel invokes request access, add a room, publish a pointer, or end a session.","events":["The service router classifies the operation using SERVICE_CHANGE_OPERATIONS.","Change-only transaction, approval, or CSRF-independent service handling is selected."],"outcomes":["One implementation includes every state mutation, including request access and session registration/end.","Another includes only the operations R0-99 calls state-transition: add room, publish pointer and withdraw pointer."],"observer":"Kernel and the owner see different transaction or approval behaviour for the same operation."}},{"subject":"REQUEST_ID_UNPRINTABLE_CATEGORIES","kind":"undefined text vocabulary","text":"REQUEST_ID_UNPRINTABLE_CATEGORIES","question":"Which Unicode general categories are unprintable, which Unicode version supplies category assignments, and does Registry reject a request id containing any such code point or apply another rule?","options":["A fixed set of Unicode general-category labels, with any matching code point refused","A version-dependent use of the runtime's isprintable classification","An ASCII-only printable request-id rule"],"default_guess":"Declare a fixed set of Unicode general-category labels and reject a request id containing any code point in them, without relying on the runtime's moving isprintable definition.","witness":{"initial":"A caller supplies a nonblank request id containing a format or control code point and otherwise fewer than 128 characters.","events":["Registry validates the request id before creating a record."],"outcomes":["An implementation using a fixed category set refuses it as unprintable.","An implementation using runtime isprintable, ASCII-only rules, or a different category set accepts it or refuses a different set of characters."],"observer":"The caller sees the create accepted or refused."}},{"subject":"data_provider model dependencies for the added grouped constants","kind":"dependency ambiguity","text":"`models`, for exactly the row models and enumerations of its eight tables:

Its other constants are scalars and import nothing.","question":"Are CUSTOMER_VALUE_FAILURE_KINDS, PROJECT_STATUS_CONFIRMATIONS, OWNER_NAVIGATION_ACTIONS, OWNER_CHANGE_ACTIONS and SERVICE_CHANGE_OPERATIONS scalars with no model imports, or typed collections that directly name model enumerations; if they are collections, which provider-owned model symbols must data_provider import?","options":["Treat them as typed collections and add their directly named enums to the models dependency","Encode their members as untyped strings so they import no models","Derive them from the eight existing tables and export no additional stored collections"],"default_guess":"Treat them as typed immutable collections and explicitly add only the enumeration symbols they directly name to data_provider's models dependency."}]}
